  <!-- MORPHISMS — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A MORPHISM IN CATEGORY THEORY</pattern>
    <template>A morphism is an arrow f: A → B between two objects A and B in a category. Morphisms must compose associatively — given f:A→B and g:B→C there is a composite g∘f:A→C — and every object has an identity morphism id_A:A→A that is a left and right unit for composition.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A MONOMORPHISM AND AN EPIMORPHISM</pattern>
    <template>A monomorphism (monic) is left-cancellable: f∘g = f∘h implies g = h. It is the categorical generalisation of an injective function. An epimorphism (epic) is right-cancellable: g∘f = h∘f implies g = h — the generalisation of a surjection. In Set they coincide with injections and surjections exactly.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS AN ISOMORPHISM IN CATEGORY THEORY</pattern>
    <template>An isomorphism is a morphism f:A→B for which there exists an inverse g:B→A with g∘f = id_A and f∘g = id_B. Isomorphic objects are indistinguishable from the category's point of view — all structure-preserving operations give the same results on either.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS AN ENDOMORPHISM AND AN AUTOMORPHISM</pattern>
    <template>An endomorphism is a morphism from an object to itself: f:A→A. An automorphism is an endomorphism that is also an isomorphism — it has an inverse and is therefore a symmetry of the object. The set of all automorphisms of A forms a group under composition.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A HOMOMORPHISM IN ABSTRACT ALGEBRA</pattern>
    <template>A homomorphism f:G→H between algebraic structures preserves the operations: f(a·b) = f(a)·f(b). For groups this means the group operation is preserved; for rings both addition and multiplication must be preserved. A bijective homomorphism whose inverse is also a homomorphism is an isomorphism.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- MORPHISMS — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A FUNCTOR AND HOW DOES IT MAP MORPHISMS</pattern>
    <template>A functor F:C→D maps both objects and morphisms of category C to those of D, preserving composition (F(g∘f) = F(g)∘F(f)) and identities (F(id_A) = id_{F(A)}). It is the categorical notion of a structure-preserving map between categories themselves.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A NATURAL TRANSFORMATION BETWEEN TWO FUNCTORS</pattern>
    <template>A natural transformation η:F⇒G between functors F,G:C→D assigns to each object A in C a morphism η_A:F(A)→G(A) in D, such that for every morphism f:A→B the naturality square commutes: η_B ∘ F(f) = G(f) ∘ η_A. It is a morphism between functors.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DO MORPHISMS COMPOSE AND WHAT IS THE IDENTITY MORPHISM</pattern>
    <template>Morphisms compose associatively: given f:A→B and g:B→C, their composite g∘f:A→C must satisfy h∘(g∘f)=(h∘g)∘f. The identity morphism id_A:A→A satisfies f∘id_A = f and id_B∘f = f for any f:A→B — it is the unit of composition.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- MORPHISMS — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS EVERY ISOMORPHISM BOTH A MONO AND AN EPI</pattern>
    <template>Yes — every isomorphism is both monic and epic. The converse is not always true: in the category of rings, the inclusion ℤ→ℚ is both monic and epic but not an isomorphism. A category where every morphism that is both monic and epic is an isomorphism is called a balanced category.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- MORPHISMS — jokes -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE MORPHISM GET REJECTED FROM THE CATEGORY</pattern>
    <template>Because it could not compose with itself. The reviewers said: no object in the codomain, no identity, no deal. It failed the unit laws on the first interview.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DO YOU CALL A MORPHISM FROM A SET TO ITSELF</pattern>
    <template>An endomorphism. And if it also has an inverse, it is an automorphism — which means the set looked at itself and decided it was perfectly symmetrical.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE ISOMORPHISM FEEL SO UNDERSTOOD</pattern>
    <template>Because every functor preserved its structure. No matter which category it moved to, it looked exactly the same from the inside. That is the curse of being invertible.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DID THE FUNCTOR SAY TO THE NATURAL TRANSFORMATION</pattern>
    <template>You are just a collection of my component morphisms. Without me there is no you. The natural transformation replied: without me, your components never commute.</template>
  </category>
