  <!-- FIXED POINTS — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A FIXED POINT OF A FUNCTION</pattern>
    <template>A fixed point of a function f is a value x where f(x) = x — the function maps the point to itself. Every continuous self-map of a compact convex set has at least one fixed point (Brouwer); every contraction mapping on a complete metric space has exactly one (Banach).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE Y COMBINATOR AND WHAT DOES IT COMPUTE</pattern>
    <template>The Y combinator Y = λf.(λx.f(x x))(λx.f(x x)) is a fixed-point combinator in the untyped lambda calculus: Y f = f (Y f). It computes the fixed point of f, enabling recursion without explicit self-reference — feeding the function to itself until a base case stops the expansion.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS BANACH S FIXED POINT THEOREM</pattern>
    <template>The Banach fixed-point theorem states that every contraction mapping f on a non-empty complete metric space has a unique fixed point, reachable by iterating f from any starting point. A contraction is a map where d(f(x),f(y)) ≤ k·d(x,y) for some k &lt; 1 — each application brings points closer together.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS BROUWER S FIXED POINT THEOREM</pattern>
    <template>Brouwer's fixed-point theorem states that every continuous function from a closed convex compact set (like a disk or ball) to itself has at least one fixed point. It relies on the topological property that such sets have no holes — the proof uses the fact that the identity map on the boundary cannot be continuously extended to a retraction.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FIXED POINTS — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES FIX F GIVE RECURSIVE DATA TYPES</pattern>
    <template>Fix F is the type satisfying Fix F ≅ F(Fix F) — the least fixed point of the functor F. For F X = 1 + A×X this gives the list type; for F X = 1 + A×X×X this gives binary trees. The isomorphism is witnessed by two morphisms: in (F(Fix F)→Fix F) and out (Fix F→F(Fix F)), forming an F-algebra and F-coalgebra respectively.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES MU RELATE TO INDUCTION AND NU TO COINDUCTION</pattern>
    <template>The least fixed point μF is the initial F-algebra: it is defined by induction — you build it from the bottom up by applying F to the empty set repeatedly. The greatest fixed point νF is the terminal F-coalgebra: it is defined by coinduction — you observe it from the outside, and it supports infinite structures like streams that are defined by their observations rather than their construction.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES KLEENE S FIXED POINT THEOREM BUILD THE LEAST FIXED POINT</pattern>
    <template>Kleene's theorem states that on a complete partial order with bottom ⊥, the least fixed point of a Scott-continuous function f is the supremum of the chain ⊥ ⊑ f(⊥) ⊑ f(f(⊥)) ⊑ … — the limit of iterating f from the bottom. This is how denotational semantics defines the meaning of recursive programs.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS LAWVERE S FIXED POINT THEOREM</pattern>
    <template>Lawvere's fixed-point theorem states that in a cartesian closed category, if there exists a surjective morphism e: A → (A→B) then every endomorphism on B has a fixed point. It is the categorical core of Cantor's theorem, Gödel's incompleteness theorem, and the undecidability of the halting problem — all are instances of a diagonal argument.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FIXED POINTS — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS THE Y COMBINATOR TYPEABLE IN THE SIMPLY TYPED LAMBDA CALCULUS</pattern>
    <template>No — the Y combinator is not typeable in the simply typed lambda calculus. The sub-expression λx.f(x x) requires x to have type τ and also type τ→σ simultaneously, which is impossible in a simple type system. This is why simply typed lambda calculus is strongly normalising — it cannot express general recursion, only terminating computation.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FIXED POINTS — jokes -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE FIXED POINT REFUSE TO MOVE</pattern>
    <template>Because by definition, it was already exactly where the function needed it to be. Moving would make it a non-fixed point, and then it would cease to exist as the thing it was.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DID F OF X SAY WHEN X EQUALLED F OF X</pattern>
    <template>f(x) said: I am perfectly happy. x replied: that is literally the definition of me. They stayed there together forever — a fixed point of the conversation.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY IS THE Y COMBINATOR BAD AT THERAPY</pattern>
    <template>Because it keeps applying itself recursively to the problem without ever reaching a base case. The therapist asked: how does that make you feel? The Y combinator said: how does that make you feel? how does that make you feel? how does that make—</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DID BROUWER SAY TO THE FUNCTION WITH NO FIXED POINT</pattern>
    <template>You cannot exist. If you are continuous and you map this compact convex set to itself, you must fix at least one point. The function argued it was a counterexample. Brouwer said: then you are not continuous. The function had to admit it had a hole.</template>
  </category>
